.RB *fineline_curses_alloc (void);
.br
.B char
.RB *fineline_curses (fineline_t
.IB fine ", const char *prompt);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The fineline library is a replacement for the GNU ReadLine library.
The main differences are:
.IP " \(bu" 3em
Fineline can run in an ncursesw window, in addition to reading from a plain tty.
This is the main justification for this library.
.IP " \(bu" 3em
Fineline uses common editing commands such as ^C/^V for copy/paste,
^F/^R for forward/backward searches, ^Z/^Y for undo/redo, and so on.
The complete list is included later in this page.
.IP " \(bu" 3em
Fineline's name completion mechanism has a cleaner interface.
.IP " \(bu" 3em
Fineline supports multi-line inputs,
syntax coloring,
hinting (such as showing function arguments), and
asynchronous chat.
.P
Its editing capabilities are good enough that it can make a decent text editor.
That's what the "fled" program is.

.SH "USER EDIT COMMANDS"
The cursor keypad keys do what you would expect.
On multi-line inputs,
the up and down keys move from line to line within the input,
except moving off the top or bottom will jump to a history entry.
Using <Ctrl> with a side arrow key moves by words instead of characters.
Using <Ctrl> with an up/down arrow key moves through history even on multi-line inputs.
.P
Using <Shift> with any movement key will start or extend a text selection.
You can then use <Ctrl-X> to delete it, <Ctrl-C> to copy it.
.P
The other commands are all control characters.
These are:
.TS
c l
<Ctrl-@>	--
<Ctrl-A>	Select all, leaving cursor at end.
<Ctrl-B>	Bounce between current input and history
<Ctrl-C>	Copy selected text to the cut buffer
<Ctrl-D>	End input, exit program
<Ctrl-E>	Invoke an external editor
<Ctrl-F>	PROMPT for "find" text, and search forward
<Ctrl-G>	PROMPT for a line number or function name, and go there
<Ctrl-H>	<Backspace>
<Ctrl-I>	<Tab> - name completion
<Ctrl-J>	Linefeed, same as <Enter>
<Ctrl-K>	Prompt for a configuration setting.
<Ctrl-L>	<FormFeed> - redraw the input from scratch
<Ctrl-M>	<Enter>, either process the line or insert a newline
<Ctrl-N>	Repeat search in forward direction
<Ctrl-O>	--
<Ctrl-P>	Repeat search in reverse direction
<Ctrl-Q>	Quit editing a file without saving it (in "fled" editor)
<Ctrl-R>	PROMPT for "find" text, and search backward
<Ctrl-S>	Save a file and quit (in "fled" editor)
<Ctrl-T>	--
<Ctrl-U>	Delete to start of line or start of input
<Ctrl-V>	Paste
<Ctrl-W>	Delete to start of word.
<Ctrl-X>	Cut selected text
<Ctrl-Y>	Redo
<Ctrl-Z>	Undo
<Ctrl-[>	<Esc> Save to history without processing, and then start a new input
<Ctrl-\e>	Quote the next character (insert it even if Ctrl or cursor key)
<Ctrl-]>	--
<Ctrl-^>	--
<Ctrl-_>	Expand the selection to whole lines
.TE

.SH "BASIC TTY USAGE"
The simplest way to use fineline is by calling the
.RI fineline( prompt )
function.
This is intended to be a drop-in replacement for GNU readline().
It displays the prompt, reads a line, and returns it as a dynamically-allocated
string, which the calling program is responsible for freeing.
.P
.nf
	#include <stdio.h>    /* for puts() */
	#include <stdlib.h>   /* for free() */
	#include <fineline.h>

	int main(int argc, char **argv)
	{
		char	*line;

		while ((line = fineline("Test> ") != NULL) {
			puts(line);
			free(line);
		}
		return 0;
	}
.fi
.P
Most of the more sophisticated features such as syntax coloring require
setting options or pointers to callback functions.
These are stored in a
.B fineline_t
struct.
For simplicity's sake, the fineline() function uses an internal
.B fineline_t
which isn't available to you.
You'll need to allocate one via fineline_tty_alloc(), configure it, use
.RI fineline_tty( fine ", " prompt )
to read lines, and then
.RI fineline_free( fine )
to free it.
.P
.nf
	#include <stdio.h>
	#include <stdlib.h>
	#include <fineline.h>

	int main(int argc, char *argv)
	{
		fineline_t *fine;
		char	*line;

		/* Allocate a fineline_t */
		fine = fineline_tty_alloc();

		/* Initialize the fineline_t.  You can store function
		 * pointers in it, set options, load history, etc.
		 */
		fineline_history_load(fine, "history");

		/* Do the prompting */
		while ((line = fineline_tty(fine, "Test> ")) != NULL) {
			puts(line);
			free(line);
		}

		/* Save the history to a file */
		fineline_history_save(fine, "history");

		/* Clean up */
		fineline_free(fine);

		return 0;
	}
.fi

.SH "BASIC CURSES USAGE"
This is specifically designed to work with the
.BR ncurses (3)
version of the curses full-screen library.
Since fineline supports UTF-8 text, you should specifically uses the
"ncursesw" variant.
.P
Usage is very similar to second the "tty" example above,
except "tty" in function names is replaced by "curses"
so you'll be calling fineline_curses_alloc() and
.RI fineline_curses( fine ).
.P
In order to avoid linking dependencies when using the shared library
version of fineline,
the curses-dependent functions are not compiled into the library.
They're defined in the fineline.h header, but only if FINELINE_CURSES and
NCURSES_WIDE are defined.
NCURSES_WIDE should be defined in the ncursesw header file, but you'll need
to define FINELINE_CURSES yourself.
If your program is split across multiple source files, you should only
define FINELINE_CURSES in one of them, to avoid redefining the functions.
.P
.nf
	#include <ncursesw/curses.h>
	#define FINELINE_CURSES
	#include <fineline.h>

	int main(int argc, char **argv)
	{
		fineline_t *fine;
		char *line;

		initscr();
		raw();
		noecho();

		fine = fineline_curses_alloc();
		fineline_history_load(fine, "history");

		while ((line = fineline_curses(stdscr, fine, "Test> ")) != NULL) {
			addstr(line);
			addch('\n');
			free(line);
		}

		fineline_history_save(fine, "history");
		fineline_free(fine);
		return 0;
	}
.fi

